      "content": "From a strictly historical, quantitative risk perspective, the sheer number of competing ethnic interests does increase the probabilistic challenge for long-term stability compared to more homogenous nations. This structural complexity inherently demands more robust and costly governance mechanisms to prevent fragmentation from escalating into conflict. Therefore, analysts often flag this high degree of internal competition as a fundamental, elevated systemic risk. This suggests that while good institutions are the cure, the underlying fragmented structure necessitates higher risk premiums in your models.",
